narrative:
## Anatomy of a Safe Library

A safe library has three characteristics: every public function has contracts, every type has invariants, every edge case is handled:

### Math Utilities
```xiom
fn safe_sqrt(x: Float64) -> Float64
  requires: x >= 0.0;
  ensures: result >= 0.0;
{
  return math.sqrt(x);
}

fn safe_log(x: Float64) -> Float64
  requires: x > 0.0;
{
  return math.log(x);
}
```

### String Validators
```xiom
type TrimmedStr = {
  value: Str;
  invariant: string.str_len(value) > 0;
}

fn non_empty(s: Str) -> Option[TrimmedStr] {
  let trimmed = string.str_trim(s);
  if string.str_len(trimmed) == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(TrimmedStr{ value: trimmed });
}
```

### Data Structures
```xiom
type BoundedArray = {
  items: Vec[Int];
  max_size: Int;
  invariant: items.len() <= max_size;
}

fn BoundedArray.push(self: &mut Self, item: Int)
  requires: self.items.len() < self.max_size;
  ensures: self.items.len() == self@pre.items.len() + 1;
{
  self.items.push(item);
}
```

### The Module Pattern
Group these in a module. Import what you need. Every import brings its contracts with it:
```xiom
module safe_lib {
  pub type TrimmedStr = ...;
  pub fn safe_sqrt(...) ...;
  pub fn non_empty(...) ...;
}
```

Callers of `safe_lib` get safety guarantees without reading the source — the contracts ARE the documentation.

